Case Study: Dementia of the Alzheimer's Type
History and Physical
Max, a 76-year-old male, presented to a neurologist on consult from his primary physician as an outpatient. The patient was brought in by his wife who reported problems with simple calculations, telling time, and home repair tasks, which were normally simple and routine for him to do.
Past Medical History: Cardiomyopathy; cortical atrophy noted on last magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with enlarged sulci; hypercholesterolemia; family reported difficulty with numbers and time for approximately 6 months prior to this admission.
Social History: Self-employed in a trucking business; married with two grown children in the area. Some postsecondary education (associate's degree); naval officer during WWII. Family reported that patient is "less moody" over the past few months.
Surgical History: Hernia repair.
Assessment Results
Expression
Automatic Speech: WFL
Repetition Ability: WFL
Lexical Retrieval-Naming: Naming difficulties noted; specifically names of people and locations; poor confrontation naming.
Conversational Ability: Common social phrases were used appropriately; able to express his wants and needs at the sentence level; able to engage in a one-on-one dialogue that was concrete, and context driven (contextualized language); narrative discourse focused on two topics: lack of income and returning to work; patient could be redirected with verbal cue.
Pragmatic Skills: WFL
Paraphasias: Semantic paraphasias noted in conversation.
Rate: WFL
Intelligibility: WFL
Prosody: WFL
Articulation: WFL
Fluency: WFL
Auditory Comprehension
Answering Yes/No Questions: Answers to abstract yes/no questions were mildly impaired.
Executing Commands: WFL executing one-step commands; following complex multistep commands was mild-moderately impaired.
Understanding Stories & Paragraphs: WFL for short paragraphs up to 5 sentences in length. Errors increase as paragraph length increases.
Understanding Conversational Speech: Sentence comprehension was WFL; comprehension at the conversational level was variable depending on the number of people involved and the complexity of the information.
Identifying Objects & Their Functions: WFL
Word-level Comprehension: WFL
Sentence-level Comprehension: Good for simple declarative sentences; moderately impaired as the semantic and syntactic complexity increased, e.g., connectives and clausal relationships.
Reading
Oral Reading: WFL for simple, commonly used high frequency words and simple declarative sentences.
Reading Comprehension: Good for simple sentences but moderately impaired at the more complex paragraph level.
Oral Spelling: Moderately impaired for irregularly spelled words and polysyllabic words.
Written Expression
Copying: Able to copy words and sentences.
Writing to Dictation: Able to write sentences to dictation.
Self-generated: Writes functionally at the phrase level to express his needs if necessary.
Written Spelling: Moderately impaired for irregularly spelled words and polysyllabic words characterized by missing letters.
Drawing: Not functional for communication purposes and often ended up in perseverative "doodles."
Cognition
Alertness: Level of alertness during daytime hours was WFL; sometimes awakens fully alert and agitated in the middle of the night asking to go to his adult day-care program.
Attention/Concentration: Mild-moderately impaired raising the issue of safety at home and in public.
Visuospatial Skills: Patient was unable to consistently recognize family members and thought they were impostors.
Memory: Semantic memory is moderately impaired; moderately-severely impaired episodic memory for recent events; procedural memory is functional for overlearned tasks, e.g., gardening, minor home repairs, meal preparation.
Executive Functions: Relied on family members to make appointments; unable to plan and organize a daily task independently; poor judgment for home safety scenarios.
Behavioral Symptoms
Deficit Awareness: "There is something wrong with my brain. It's like there's a hole in it."
Frustration: Agitation and anxiety noted; more agitated at night.
Emotional Lability: Not noted.
Current Personality Characteristics: Premorbidly rigid, confident, assertive and rule-bound (ex-military officer); currently, more flexible.
Functional Communication Profile
Max was diagnosed with Dementia of the Alzheimer's Type (DAT). His ability to understand and use language is intact for his ADL needs. However, he has difficulty formulating language to discuss recent events and facts, dates and time, facts of general knowledge related to his personal history, and political and social history of the times. His semantic memory is also beginning to show signs of decline, manifested by semantic paraphasias. There are concomitant word-finding problems.
Max's procedural memory is still intact, so he can perform basic meal preparation, gardening, and minor home repairs. However, due to his attentional deficits, his safety at home and in public can be compromised. This can lead to dangerous errors while executing ADLs. He tries to participate in conversations but demonstrates frustration when he is unable to do so effectively. As a consequence, he withdraws into silence. His language is composed primarily of idioms, common social phrases, and stereotypic utterances.
Max presents with Capgras syndrome (also known as "impostor syndrome") (Josephs, 2007), which is beginning to affect his relationship with his wife because he thinks she is an impostor, not the woman he married. This creates significant stress for his primary caregiver and impacts the quality of their interactions. Despite this challenge, his wife remains engaged in his care and is interested in helping him.
Because of his memory impairments and inability to drive safely, Max is very dependent on his wife for transportation to appointments. He often wakes his wife during the night thinking that it is "time to leave." When out in the community, he often becomes impatient and has difficulty staying in one place for more than 15 minutes.
Max has some awareness of his deficits and told his daughter, "There's something wrong in my head." However, this limited awareness is insufficient to support independent problem-solving or safety judgment. His cognitive deficits, rather than linguistic problems, are the primary barrier to his functional independence.
Goal Setting
Initial Goal-Setting Session
During the collaborative goal-setting session, Max, his wife (primary caregiver), and the speech-language pathologist discussed priorities. Max expressed desire to "get back to work" and frustration about "not being able to fix things like I used to." His wife emphasized concerns about his safety when attempting home repairs, his night-time confusion, and the emotional toll of the Capgras syndrome. She also noted that their social isolation has increased as Max becomes uncomfortable in group settings.
Patient-Identified Priorities
Continue doing home repairs and gardening
Remember important information and appointments
Be able to talk with friends at the senior center
Stay in his own home
Family-Identified Priorities
Ensure safety during daily activities
Reduce night-time confusion and wandering
Manage Capgras syndrome episodes
Maintain meaningful communication despite cognitive decline
Access respite and support services
Long-Term Goals with Clinical Reasoning
Goal 1: Functional Safety Awareness
Max will demonstrate the ability to select the correct written solution when given home safety scenarios with 80% accuracy.
Clinical Reasoning: Max's impaired executive function and attention create significant safety risks during previously routine tasks. His desire to maintain independence through home repairs conflicts with his reduced safety judgment. This goal addresses both his need for autonomy and family's safety concerns by using his intact reading comprehension for simple text to support decision-making.
Goal 2: Supported Communication Participation
Max will participate in structured conversations using a memory book and environmental supports for 10-minute interactions.
Clinical Reasoning: Max's episodic memory impairment and word-finding difficulties cause him to withdraw from social interactions. His preserved procedural memory and ability to use contextualized language can be leveraged through external supports. This goal maintains his social connections while accommodating his cognitive limitations.
Goal 3: Structured ADL Completion
Max will follow written and pictorial instructions for familiar tasks (home repairs, landscaping) with 75% accuracy.
Clinical Reasoning: Max's intact procedural memory for overlearned tasks can be supported through external cues. His military background makes him responsive to structured instructions. This goal allows continued engagement in meaningful activities while ensuring safety through step-by-step guidance.
Goal 4: Environmental Navigation
Max will use visual and written cues to orient to time, place, and daily routine with 70% accuracy.
Clinical Reasoning: Night-time confusion and daytime disorientation create stress for both Max and his wife. His ability to read simple text and respond to visual cues can be used to reduce confusion. This goal targets both safety and caregiver burden while maintaining Max's dignity.
Goal 5: Family-Centered Care Optimization
Family members will demonstrate use of communication strategies from the FOCUSED program in 80% of observed interactions.
Clinical Reasoning: As Max's primary support system, family members need evidence-based strategies to facilitate his participation. The progressive nature of DAT requires family education for long-term management. This goal ensures sustainable care while reducing caregiver stress.
Short-Term Goals (Aligned with Long-Term Goals and Treatment Components)
STG 1: Safety Recognition (Supports LTG 1)
Max will identify unsafe situations in picture cards with 85% accuracy given verbal cues.
Timeline: Weeks 1-4 of safety awareness training
Measurement: Percentage correct on 10-item safety scenarios
Corresponds to: Treatment Target 1, Recognition Phase
Goal Attainment Scale (GAS):
-2 (Much less than expected): Identifies <40% of unsafe situations even with maximum cues
-1 (Less than expected): Identifies 40-60% of unsafe situations with moderate support
0 (Expected outcome): Identifies unsafe situations with 85% accuracy with verbal cues
+1 (More than expected): Independently identifies 90% of unsafe situations
+2 (Much more than expected): Generalizes safety awareness to novel situations >85%
STG 2: Memory Book Use (Supports LTG 2)
Max will refer to memory book during conversations with 75% consistency when cued.
Timeline: Weeks 1-3 of communication intervention
Measurement: Frequency of memory book reference during 5-minute conversations
Corresponds to: Treatment Target 2, Training Phase
Goal Attainment Scale (GAS):
-2 (Much less than expected): References book <25% with maximal cueing
-1 (Less than expected): Uses book 25-50% with frequent verbal prompts
0 (Expected outcome): References memory book 75% when cued
+1 (More than expected): Spontaneously uses book 60% of opportunities
+2 (Much more than expected): Independently uses book throughout day >70%
STG 3: Instruction Following (Supports LTG 3)
Max will complete 3-step familiar tasks using written/pictorial guides with 80% accuracy.
Timeline: Weeks 1-4 of procedural memory support training
Measurement: Steps completed correctly during gardening/repair tasks
Corresponds to: Treatment Target 3, Structured Practice Phase
Goal Attainment Scale (GAS):
-2 (Much less than expected): Completes <40% of steps even with physical assistance
-1 (Less than expected): Completes 40-60% of steps with verbal and visual support
0 (Expected outcome): Completes 3-step tasks with 80% accuracy using guides
+1 (More than expected): Completes 4-step tasks with 75% accuracy
+2 (Much more than expected): Generalizes guide use to unfamiliar tasks >70%
STG 4: Temporal Orientation (Supports LTG 4)
Max will correctly identify time of day and next scheduled activity using visual schedule with 70% accuracy.
Timeline: Weeks 1-3 of orientation training
Measurement: Correct responses to orientation questions 3x daily
Corresponds to: Treatment Target 4, Environmental Structuring Phase
Goal Attainment Scale (GAS):
-2 (Much less than expected): Oriented <30% even with multiple cues
-1 (Less than expected): Achieves 30-50% orientation with visual and verbal support
0 (Expected outcome): Correctly oriented 70% using visual schedule
+1 (More than expected): Maintains orientation 80% with minimal schedule reference
+2 (Much more than expected): Spontaneously uses environmental cues for orientation >75%
STG 5: Family Strategy Implementation (Supports LTG 5)
Wife will use simplified language and allow processing time in 80% of interactions.
Timeline: Weeks 1-2 of FOCUSED caregiver training
Measurement: Observation checklist during natural interactions
Corresponds to: Treatment Target 5, Initial Caregiver Education Phase
Goal Attainment Scale (GAS):
-2 (Much less than expected): Uses strategies <30% of observed interactions
-1 (Less than expected): Implements strategies 30-60% with coaching
0 (Expected outcome): Uses strategies in 80% of interactions
+1 (More than expected): Independently modifies strategies based on Max's responses
+2 (Much more than expected): Teaches strategies to other family members effectively
Treatment Approaches Using RTSS Framework
Treatment Target 1: Safety Awareness Training
Active Ingredients:
Errorless learning with safety scenarios - Preventing incorrect response patterns through supported decision-making
Spaced retrieval for safety rules - Systematic practice intervals for encoding critical safety information
Environmental modification progression - Gradually increasing naturalistic safety challenges
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 10 safety scenario trials per session
Frequency: 3x/week
Duration: 30-minute sessions
Cumulative Intensity: 36 sessions total
Progressive Complexity Levels:
Simple hazard identification (hot stove, wet floor)
Two-choice safety decisions with pictures
Multi-step safety sequences for home tasks
Generalization to community settings
Mechanism of Action: Errorless learning preserves remaining cognitive resources by preventing confusion from errors. Spaced retrieval strengthens encoding in compromised memory systems. Environmental modifications provide external executive support while maintaining functional independence.
Treatment Target 2: Memory Book Implementation
Active Ingredients:
Personalized memory aid development - Creating meaningful, individualized reference materials
Behavioral chaining for book use - Breaking memory book use into manageable steps
Conversational coaching protocol - Structured practice using book during interactions
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 5 conversation trials with memory book per session
Frequency: 2x/week
Duration: 45-minute sessions
Cumulative Intensity: 24 sessions total
Progressive Complexity Levels:
Finding specific pages (family, daily schedule)
Using book to answer simple questions
Referencing book during brief conversations
Independent use during extended interactions
Mechanism of Action: External memory system compensates for episodic memory loss while utilizing preserved procedural memory. Behavioral chaining establishes automatic reference patterns. Repeated practice in natural contexts promotes generalization despite cognitive decline.
Treatment Target 3: Procedural Task Support
Active Ingredients:
Visual instruction templates - Step-by-step pictorial guides for familiar tasks
Procedural memory activation - Leveraging intact motor memories with external cues
Task segmentation protocol - Breaking complex tasks into single-step components
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 2 complete task sequences per session
Frequency: 3x/week
Duration: 30-minute sessions
Cumulative Intensity: 36 sessions total
Progressive Complexity Levels:
Single-step tasks with picture (watering plants)
Three-step sequences with written/picture combination
Complex familiar tasks with checklist support
Modified tasks with safety adaptations
Mechanism of Action: Visual templates bypass impaired verbal memory while engaging preserved visuospatial processing. Procedural memory activation uses intact motor programs triggered by environmental cues. Task segmentation reduces cognitive load to match current capacity.
Treatment Target 4: Environmental Orientation System
Active Ingredients:
Multi-modal orientation cues - Visual, written, and auditory reminders for time/place
Structured daily routine establishment - Consistent patterns to support temporal orientation
Night-time safety protocol - Specific environmental modifications for sundowning
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: Orientation checks 5x per session with decreasing support
Frequency: Daily (7x/week)
Duration: 15-minute sessions
Cumulative Intensity: Ongoing daily intervention
Progressive Complexity Levels:
Basic day/night discrimination with lighting cues
Time of day with clock and activity pictures
Daily schedule with next activity highlighted
Week/month orientation with calendar support
Mechanism of Action: Multi-modal cues engage multiple sensory channels to compensate for memory deficits. Routine establishment creates predictable patterns reducing confusion. Environmental modifications provide continuous orientation support without caregiver burden.
Treatment Target 5: Caregiver Communication Training (FOCUSED Program)
Active Ingredients:
FOCUSED strategy instruction - Face-to-face, Orientation, Continuity, Unsticking, Structure, Exchange, Direct
Role-play with coaching - Practice implementing strategies with immediate feedback
Problem-solving for challenging behaviors - Developing individualized responses to Capgras syndrome
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 30 minutes direct training + 30 minutes supervised practice
Frequency: 1x/week
Duration: 60-minute sessions
Cumulative Intensity: 8-week program with monthly follow-up
Progressive Complexity Levels:
Single strategy implementation (e.g., Face-to-face)
Combining 2-3 strategies in interaction
Full FOCUSED protocol during daily care
Adapting strategies for specific challenges (Capgras episodes)
Mechanism of Action: Evidence-based FOCUSED strategies reduce communication breakdowns through environmental and behavioral modifications. Role-play with coaching establishes caregiver confidence and competence. Problem-solving develops sustainable management strategies for progressive symptoms.
Compensatory Strategies
Environmental Modifications:
Large-print clocks and calendars in every room
Night lights with motion sensors for bathroom path
Labels with pictures on cabinets and drawers
Simplified tool collection with safety locks
External Memory Aids:
Memory book with family photos and names
Daily schedule board with moveable markers
Voice-recorded reminders from wife
Simplified written instructions for routine tasks
Communication Supports:
"Communication card" explaining Capgras syndrome for visitors
Picture cards for common needs/wants
Structured conversation topics list for visitors
Visual choice boards for preferences
Safety Systems:
Door alarms for night-time wandering
Medication dispensers with alarms
Emergency contact card in wallet
GPS tracking device for community outings
Family/Caregiver Education
Max's wife and adult children should receive comprehensive education about:
Understanding DAT: Progressive nature, expected changes, maintaining hope within realistic expectations
Communication Strategies: Speaking slowly, using simple sentences, allowing processing time, avoiding corrections
Managing Capgras Syndrome: Not arguing about identity, redirecting to pleasant topics, using emotional validation
Safety Considerations: Removing dangerous tools, securing medications, preventing wandering
Behavioral Management: Establishing routines, reducing environmental stressors, managing sundowning
Self-Care for Caregivers: Importance of respite, support groups, maintaining own health
Training should emphasize that Max's changed behaviors are disease-related, not personal. The family needs ongoing support as they adjust to each stage of decline while preserving Max's dignity and quality of life.
Prognosis
Given Max's diagnosis of DAT with moderate cognitive impairment and relatively preserved language function, the prognosis is for continued gradual decline over several years. With appropriate environmental modifications and family support, Max can likely:
Short-term (6-12 months):
Maintain current level of communication with supports
Continue supervised engagement in familiar activities
Remain at home with increasing supervision
Medium-term (1-2 years):
Require 24-hour supervision
Communicate basic needs with difficulty
Need assistance with most ADLs
Long-term considerations:
Progressive loss of language function
Eventual need for skilled nursing facility
Focus shifting to comfort and quality of life
Protective factors include:
Strong family support system
Retained procedural memory for familiar tasks
Some deficit awareness
Responsiveness to structured interventions
The treatment focus will progressively shift from remediation to compensation to accommodation as the disease advances. Regular reassessment every 3-6 months will guide treatment modifications. Family education and support will become increasingly central to care planning.␀␀␀␀␀␀A␀␀␀␀␀Case Study: Dementia of the Alzheimer's Type
