Long-Term Goals with Clinical Reasoning
Goal 1: Improved Speech Intelligibility Through Prosodic Control
Michael will increase speech intelligibility to 75-80% for familiar listeners and 65-70% for unfamiliar listeners by reducing scanning prosody, normalizing stress patterns, and controlling rate.
Clinical Reasoning: Scanning prosody (excess equal stress) is the most stigmatizing feature of ataxic dysarthria. Prosodic abnormalities significantly reduce intelligibility and naturalness. While cerebellar damage cannot be reversed, compensatory strategies targeting prosody can improve functional communication. Rate control and stress modification can partially compensate for irregular timing. Improved intelligibility will support continued teaching and reduce social stigma.
Goal 2: Rate Normalization and Timing Control
Michael will produce connected speech at controlled rate of 130-150 wpm with reduced irregular pauses and more natural timing using pacing strategies and rhythmic cueing.
Clinical Reasoning: Slow, irregular rate with prolongations contributes to reduced intelligibility and abnormal quality. External timing cues may provide structure compensating for disrupted cerebellar timing. Rate that's too slow paradoxically reduces intelligibility; optimal rate balances articulatory accuracy with natural flow. Improved timing will enhance naturalness and listener comprehension.
Goal 3: Respiratory-Phonatory Coordination
Michael will demonstrate improved breath support and phonatory stability, reducing voice tremor impact and increasing phrase length to 8-10 syllables per breath group.
Clinical Reasoning: Irregular breathing and voice tremor result from cerebellar incoordination affecting respiratory-laryngeal systems. While tremor cannot be eliminated, controlled breathing may reduce its amplitude. Improved respiratory-phonatory coordination will increase phrase length, reduce irregularities, and improve voice quality. Strategic breath management compensates for irregular breath control.
Goal 4: Classroom Communication Effectiveness
Michael will effectively communicate in classroom setting using amplification, teaching modifications, and compensatory strategies, maintaining teaching effectiveness as measured by student comprehension and evaluations.
Clinical Reasoning: Teaching is central to Michael's identity and livelihood. Losing this role would be psychologically and financially devastating. Voice amplification addresses reduced loudness and projection. Teaching modifications (visual supports, written materials, classroom management strategies) reduce communication demands. Maintaining employment provides purpose, income, and normalcy crucial for adjustment and quality of life.
Goal 5: Functional AAC System for Future Needs
Michael will establish multimodal communication system (speech + AAC + writing) with infrastructure for future progression, achieving 90% communication success in daily activities.
Clinical Reasoning: SCA3 is progressive; speech will deteriorate over time. Proactive AAC planning prevents crisis intervention later. Early introduction normalizes technology use. Michael's intact cognition makes him excellent candidate for text-based AAC. Planning while he can participate respects autonomy. AAC provides psychological security (communication backup) even when not regularly used. Voice banking preserves communication identity.
Goal 6: Management of Communication-Related Psychological Distress
Michael will demonstrate reduced anxiety and depression related to communication impairment, improved adjustment to diagnosis, and effective coping strategies through psychological support and communication success experiences.
Clinical Reasoning: Anxiety and depression significantly impact quality of life and may worsen ataxic symptoms. Anticipatory grief about progressive disease is appropriate but needs support. Successful communication reduces helplessness. Psychological intervention addressing existential concerns, relationship stress, and adjustment is essential. Addressing mental health improves engagement with all interventions.
Goal 7: Long-Term Communication Planning and Self-Management
Michael and Emily will develop comprehensive communication plan addressing current and future needs including AAC progression, workplace accommodations, and strategies for various disease stages.
Clinical Reasoning: Progressive disease requires proactive planning. Planning reduces anxiety by providing sense of control. Discussing future while Michael can participate respects autonomy and ensures communication system reflects his values and preferences. Family preparation reduces crisis situations. Education about disease trajectory allows realistic expectations and timely interventions.
Short-Term Goals
STG 1: Contrastive Stress and Prosodic Control (Supports LTG 1)
Michael will produce sentences with appropriate contrastive stress patterns (stressing target word while reducing stress on others) with 80% accuracy, reducing scanning quality.
