Treatment Approaches Using RTSS Framework
Treatment Target 1: Prosodic Control and Stress Normalization
Target: Reduced scanning prosody through normalized stress patterns
Active Ingredients:
Contrastive stress drills - Practicing stress on different words in sentences
Loudness variation exercises - Exaggerating stress on target words
Pitch variation training - Using pitch to signal stress
Rhythm and timing exercises - Normal stress-timed English patterns
Sentence-level prosody practice - Integrating natural intonation contours
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 60-80 productions per session
Frequency: 4x/week
Duration: 45 minutes
Cumulative: 48 sessions over 12 weeks
Mechanism: Ataxic dysarthria involves disrupted cerebellar timing and coordination affecting prosodic control. Excess equal stress results from inability to grade force and time movements appropriately. Contrastive stress drills provide external structure for differential stress patterns. Exaggerated practice may overcome tendency toward equal stress. Rhythmic training addresses cerebellar timing deficits.
Treatment Target 2: Rate and Timing Control
Target: Normalized speech rate with reduced irregularities
Active Ingredients:
Pacing boards - Visual/tactile syllable-by-syllable cueing
Rhythmic cueing - Metronome or rhythmic patterns
Delayed auditory feedback (DAF) - Technology-based rate control
Optimal rate identification - Finding rate balancing accuracy and naturalness
Self-monitoring training - Awareness and adjustment of rate
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 50 productions per session
Frequency: 4x/week
Duration: 30 minutes within 45-minute sessions
Cumulative: 24 hours over 12 weeks
Mechanism: Cerebellar damage disrupts internal timing mechanisms causing irregular rate with prolongations and unexpected accelerations. External timing cues (metronome, DAF) provide compensatory temporal structure. Optimal rate (not too slow, not too fast) maximizes intelligibility. Self-monitoring develops awareness allowing real-time adjustment.
Treatment Target 3: Respiratory-Phonatory Coordination
Target: Improved breath support and reduced voice tremor impact
Active Ingredients:
Controlled exhalation training - Sustained /s/ and /z/ for timing control
Optimal breath group planning - Strategic pausing at linguistic boundaries
Pitch control exercises - Finding optimal pitch where tremor less noticeable
Loudness control - Adequate volume with controlled effort
Tremor masking techniques - Environmental sound, amplification
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 40 productions per session
Frequency: 3x/week
Duration: 20-30 minutes within sessions
Cumulative: 18 hours over 12 weeks
Mechanism: Voice tremor (3-5 Hz) results from cerebellar oscillation affecting laryngeal control. While tremor cannot be eliminated behaviorally, controlled breathing may reduce amplitude. Optimal pitch selection (tremor may be less noticeable at certain pitches) and masking (amplification, environmental sound) reduce perceptual impact. Breath control compensates for irregular respiratory-phonatory coordination.
Treatment Target 4: Classroom Communication and Amplification
Target: Effective teaching communication with accommodations
Active Ingredients:
Voice amplification system - Classroom microphone and speaker setup
Teaching modifications - Visual supports, written materials, student note-takers
Classroom management strategies - Reducing need for vocal projection
Optimal positioning - Minimizing distance and background noise
Energy conservation - Pacing teaching to prevent fatigue-related decline
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 60 minutes per session including simulated teaching
Frequency: 2x/week
Duration: Throughout 12 weeks
Cumulative: 24 hours including workplace consultation
Mechanism: Amplification compensates for reduced loudness and projection without increased effort. Teaching modifications distribute communication load across multiple modalities (visual, written, verbal). Classroom management reduces vocal demands. Energy conservation prevents within-day decline. Maintaining employment provides psychological and financial benefits critical for quality of life.
Treatment Target 5: AAC and Voice Banking
Target: Multimodal communication infrastructure for current and future needs
Active Ingredients:
Text-to-speech AAC - Tablet-based application for supplemental communication
Voice banking - Recording Michael's voice extensively for future synthetic speech
Message banking - Pre-storing important messages in natural voice
Multimodal integration - Learning when/how to supplement speech with AAC
Progressive planning - Preparing for increased future needs
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 45 minutes per session
Frequency: 2x/week
Duration: 45 minutes
Cumulative: 18 hours over 12 weeks
Mechanism: SCA3 is progressive; speech will deteriorate requiring eventual AAC dependence. Early introduction normalizes technology and builds skills while cognition intact. Voice banking preserves Michael's natural voice for future synthetic speech, maintaining communication identity. Text-based AAC leverages preserved language and literacy. Proactive planning provides control and security.
Treatment Target 6: Psychological Support and Adjustment
Target: Improved coping with progressive disease and communication loss
Active Ingredients:
Grief counseling - Processing losses (future, career, abilities, fatherhood)
Anxiety management - Addressing disease progression anxiety and hypervigilance
Acceptance strategies - Living meaningfully despite progressive disease
Relationship counseling - Supporting marriage under stress
Existential therapy - Finding meaning facing shortened lifespan
SCA support group - Connecting with others with inherited ataxias
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 20 minutes per session + referral to mental health professional
Frequency: Embedded in 4x/week therapy + separate counseling
Duration: Throughout 12 weeks and ongoing
Cumulative: 12 hours embedded + ongoing mental health treatment
Mechanism: Progressive genetic disease involves profound losses requiring grief processing. Anxiety about progression can become hypervigilance worsening quality of life. Watching father's severe disability creates anticipatory grief. Genetic implications (cannot have biological children, siblings at risk) compound distress. Psychological support addresses adjustment, provides coping tools, and improves quality of life independent of physical symptoms.
Treatment Target 7: Partner Training and Future Planning
Target: Emily providing optimal support now and prepared for future
Active Ingredients:
Education about ataxic dysarthria and SCA3
Communication strategies - Active listening, patience, AAC facilitation
Future planning - Discussing progression, advance directives, communication needs
Caregiver preparation - Realistic expectations, self-care, resources
Relationship preservation - Maintaining partnership amid caregiving
Family dynamics - Navigating at-risk siblings, parental grief
Dosage Parameters:
Frequency: Biweekly couple sessions
Duration: 60-90 minutes
Cumulative: 6-8 sessions over 12 weeks + ongoing
Mechanism: Progressive disease requires family preparation. Emily will become primary communication partner and eventual caregiver. Early training optimizes current support and prepares for future. Discussing difficult topics (disease trajectory, end-of-life, caregiver burden) while Michael can participate respects autonomy. Addressing relationship stress preserves partnership. Family education reduces crisis interventions.
Compensatory Strategies
Speech Modifications
Controlled rate (130-150 wpm) using self-pacing
Exaggerated stress on key words, reduced stress on function words
Strategic pausing at phrase boundaries
Optimal pitch selection (where tremor less noticeable)
Adequate loudness without excessive effort
Shorter utterances (8-10 syllables per breath)
Self-monitoring and self-correction
Augmentative Communication
Text-to-speech AAC with Michael's banked voice (future)
Writing key words on paper/whiteboard
Typing messages (despite hand tremor/ataxia)
Gesture and pointing
Pre-written messages for common teaching phrases
PowerPoint/visual aids reducing verbal load
Communication book for backup
Environmental Modifications
Personal voice amplifier for classroom and distance communication
Classroom amplification system (microphone + speakers)
Optimal positioning (facing students, adequate lighting, close proximity)
Reduced background noise
Written materials supplementing verbal instruction
Student note-takers capturing content Michael delivers
Visual supports (slides, board work) reducing verbal demands
Teaching Modifications
Flipped classroom model (students watch videos at home, discussion in class)
More group work and independent activities (less lecturing)
Written instructions for assignments
Student presentations reducing Michael's speaking time
Online discussion boards for complex topics
Co-teaching arrangements if available
Strategic energy conservation (teach more demanding classes when fresh)
Technology Supports
Voice amplification (personal and classroom)
Speech-to-text for written communication
Video recordings of lectures (can re-record if needed)
Online learning platforms (reduces real-time speech demands)
Text-based communication with students (email, messaging)
AAC applications for supplemental communication
Accessibility features (voice control, dictation software)
Physical Supports
Seated teaching when possible (reduces balance demands)
Cane for ambulation safety
Supportive seating reducing truncal instability
Anchoring one hand during speech (may reduce tremor transmission)
Avoiding fatigue (worsens all ataxic symptoms)
Optimal medication timing (teaching during best symptom control)
Family/Caregiver Education
Nature of Ataxic Dysarthria:
Caused by cerebellar degeneration disrupting timing, coordination, and sequencing of speech movements.
Results in hallmark features: scanning prosody, irregular articulation, voice tremor, variable rate, slurred quality, and inconsistent errors.
Symptoms vary day to day, often worsening with fatigue, anxiety, or dual-task demands.
Progressive Nature of SCA3:
Slowly progressive neurodegenerative disorder.
Affects gait, balance, fine motor control, speech, swallowing, and eventually more widespread motor systems.
Speech decline is expected over time, but therapy can maximize function for as long as possible.
Speech Fatigue:
Michael’s communication degrades with prolonged speaking, emotional stress, and end-of-day fatigue.
Energy conservation and pacing improve quality and reduce exhaustion.
Preserved Cognitive-Language Skills:
Michael’s thinking, language formulation, memory, and teaching knowledge remain intact.
Dysarthria is a motor execution disorder, not a language or cognitive impairment.
Caregiver Partner Strategies (For Emily and Others)
Communication Strategies
Maintain face-to-face position with good lighting.
Reduce background noise when possible.
Confirm topic before extended conversation (“We’re talking about the insurance form, right?”).
After breakdown, use summary confirmation rather than repeated full productions.
Allow extra time; avoid rushing.
Encourage Michael to use planned supports: amplification, AAC app, strategic breath pauses.
Validate emotional frustration and avoid correcting or prompting excessively.
Supporting AAC Use
Normalize AAC as part of communication—not a last resort.
Keep device charged and accessible.
Encourage use during fatigue or complex conversations.
Practice with Michael so both partners gain confidence.
Managing Emotional Burden
Participate in couples counseling for anticipatory grief, role changes, and planning.
Attend SCA/ataxia support groups.
Develop shared communication rituals (checking in daily, problem-solving together).
Encourage breaks for both partners; listening requires cognitive effort too.
Interprofessional/Interdisciplinary Care Plan
Neurology
Ongoing monitoring of SCA3 progression, medication adjustments (amantadine, tremor management, anxiety).
Discussion of safety, fall prevention, future mobility needs.
Physical Therapy
Gait training, balance interventions, fall-prevention program.
Core stabilization for truncal ataxia to support postural stability during speech tasks.
Assistive device education (cane, future walker consideration).
Occupational Therapy
Upper extremity ataxia compensation strategies (writing supports, computer access).
Home modifications for safety (stairs, shower, kitchen).
Fatigue management and energy conservation training.
Mental Health Professional (Psychologist/LCSW/Psychiatrist)
Treatment of anxiety and depression.
Grief counseling (diagnosis, loss of abilities, anticipated decline).
Couples therapy for relational strain and role adjustment.
Genetic Counselor
Family planning discussions, sibling risk conversations, long-term implications.
Support for navigating family dynamics.
Dietitian
Monitoring weight loss from prolonged meal times.
Adaptation for early dysphagia.
ENT/Laryngology (as needed)
Evaluation of tremor severity, laryngeal integrity, and potential medical options.
Prognosis 
Positive Prognostic Indicators
Strong motivation to maintain speech.
High cognitive reserve and intact language skills.
Excellent insight and willingness to practice strategies.
Supportive spouse and workplace.
Early intervention: therapy initiated before severe decline.
Challenging Prognostic Factors
Progressive neurodegenerative nature of SCA3.
Worsening intention tremor, voice tremor, and cerebellar incoordination.
High communication demands of teaching profession.
Presence of psychological distress (anxiety/depression).
Increased fatigue impacting performance variability.
Overall Prognosis
Short-term (3–12 months):
Good potential for meaningful gains in intelligibility, prosody, rate stabilization, and communication success using compensatory methods.
High likelihood of successfully integrating AAC as supplemental communication.
Long-term (years):
Gradual decline expected; speech may become severely limited or unintelligible.
Early AAC adoption will allow continued communication independence.
Psychological support critical to maintaining quality of life.rily). Train6␀␀␀␀␀␀lies to provide appropriate cues, respond effectively to breakdowns, facilitate AAC, and manage emotional reactions significantly improves communication success and reduces frustration for everyone. Family support is strongly associated with better outcomes and quality of life.
Treatment Target 7: Psychological Support and Emotional Regulation
Target: Reduced depression, anxiety, and frustration; improved coping with communication challenges
Active Ingredients:
Psychoeducation - Understanding emotional reactions to acquired communication disorders
Coping strategy training - Managing frustration during communication breakdowns
Cognitive restructuring - Challenging catastrophic thoughts about communication
Communication success experiences - Structured activities ensuring success to build confidence
Support group participation - Connecting with others with AOS/aphasia
Referral for psychological counseling - Professional mental health support for clinical depression
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 15-20 minutes per session plus separate counseling referral
Frequency: Embedded in 4x/week therapy plus separate counseling
Duration: Throughout 12-week treatment
Cumulative: 12-16 hours embedded intervention plus separate psychological counseling
Progressive Complexity:
Psychoeducation and validation of emotional reactions
Identifying triggers for frustration and catastrophic reactions
Teaching and practicing coping strategies (breathing, cognitive reframing, strategic withdrawal)
Creating success experiences in therapy
Addressing catastrophic thoughts (all-or-nothing thinking)
Support group introduction and participation
Long-term adjustment and identity reconstruction
Mechanism: Depression, anxiety, and frustration are both consequences of communication impairment and barriers to recovery. Catastrophic reactions during communication attempts interfere with motor learning and lead to avoidance. Depression is associated with reduced therapy engagement, social isolation, and poorer outcomes. Psychological support addresses these factors, improving emotional well-being independent of speech improvement. Structured success experiences (through AAC, scripts, etc.) provide positive communication experiences, reducing learned helplessness. Support groups provide peer models of successful adjustment and normalize struggles. Addressing psychological factors improves quality of life and may enhance therapy outcomes by reducing emotional interference with motor control and increasing engagement.
Treatment Target 8: Community Reintegration and Social Participation
Target: Return to valued activities and social roles using communication supports
Active Ingredients:
Graduated exposure - Systematic return to avoided situations with increasing difficulty
Community preparation - Pre-visit communication with church, volunteer organizations about David's needs
Functional communication practice - Role-playing church greetings, volunteer tasks, family gatherings 4. Supported community outings - Initial participation with SLP or family support 5. Identity reconstruction - Finding meaningful participation even with communication limitations 6. Advocacy training - Self-advocacy for communication needs and accommodations
Dosage Parameters:
Dose: 60 minutes preparation + community outing per week
Frequency: 1-2x/week
Duration: 60-90 minutes including community outing
Cumulative: 12-24 community-based sessions over 12 weeks
Progressive Complexity:
Preparing for return (practicing scripts, planning AAC messages)
Brief, highly structured outings (coffee shop with family)
Return to church with family support
Participation in church with prepared scripts/AAC
Return to food bank volunteering with accommodations
Attending family gatherings using multimodal communication
Independent participation in valued activities
Mechanism: Social isolation and loss of valued roles contribute to depression and diminished quality of life. Avoidance prevents development of confidence and skills needed for successful participation. Graduated exposure with support allows David to experience communication success in real-world contexts, reducing avoidance and building confidence. Preparing communication partners (church community, volunteer coordinators) creates supportive environments. Script training and AAC provide tools for specific situations. Successful participation in valued activities addresses depression, provides purpose and meaning, and demonstrates that meaningful life is possible despite persistent communication impairment. Identity reconstruction acknowledges losses while building new ways of participating in valued roles.
Compensatory Strategies
Speech Modifications
Rate reduction (50-70% of normal rate initially, progressing toward 80-90%)
Syllable segregation initially, progressing to more natural prosody
Deliberate pausing at phrase boundaries
Exaggerated articulatory movements (overarticulation)
Self-cueing strategies (tapping rhythm, initial sound/letter cues, phonetic placement reminders)
Use of carrier phrases to initiate speech ("I want to say...")
Singing or melodic production for difficult words
Augmentative Communication
Text-to-speech AAC device (tablet with communication app)
Alphabet board for first-letter supplementation during speech attempts
Communication book with high-frequency words, phrases, and pictures
Writing key words (despite mild agraphia)
Gesture and natural pointing
Drawing simple pictures or symbols
Yes/no signals (thumbs up/down, head movements)
Self-Cueing Strategies
Tapping out syllables rhythmically before speaking
Writing or air-writing first letter
Phonetic placement cues (touching lips for /p/, /b/, /m/)
Visualizing articulatory position before attempting sound
Using facilitating phonetic contexts (easier sounds first)
Singing or humming melody before speaking
Starting with automatic phrase then transitioning to target ("You know... [target word]")
Environmental Modifications
Reduce time pressure—no rushing communication
Quiet environment minimizing distractions
Face-to-face positioning
Adequate lighting for visual communication cues
Having AAC devices readily accessible
Pen and paper always available
Pre-planning communication for important situations
Listener Strategies
Patient waiting—allowing multiple attempts without interruption
Watching face and gestures for visual cues
Using context to aid understanding
Offering alphabet board without taking over communication
Confirming understanding with specific yes/no questions
Accepting and valuing all communication modalities (not privileging speech)
Not finishing sentences unless requested
Responding to message content, not delivery
Family/Caregiver Education
Understanding Apraxia of Speech
Motor planning disorder—brain cannot plan and sequence the movements for speech
Not a language problem—David knows what he wants to say, understands everything, but cannot coordinate the movements
Not weakness or paralysis—oral muscles work fine for non-speech tasks (eating, blowing, smiling) but cannot coordinate for speech
Not lack of effort—the visible struggle is David working extremely hard; more effort doesn't necessarily help
Inconsistent errors—same word comes out differently each time because motor planning is disrupted, not a specific movement
Intelligence completely intact—this is purely a speech motor problem
AOS vs. Aphasia—Understanding Both
David has both AOS (severe) and aphasia (mild)
AOS affects speech movements; aphasia affects language (word-finding, sentence formation)
His language is relatively preserved—evidenced by his writing, gesture, and comprehension
Primary problem is getting words out, not formulating them
When he writes something correctly but can't say it, that's AOS, not aphasia
Understanding the Frustration
David knows exactly what he wants to say but body won't cooperate
Like knowing a piano piece but fingers won't play the right keys
Awareness makes it worse—he hears his errors and struggles
Frustration is completely understandable, not a behavior problem
Catastrophic reactions (emotional outbursts) are neurological, not personality changes
Depression is expected—communication is fundamental to identity and relationships
Communication Support Strategies
Wait patiently—give David time without rushing or interrupting attempts
Watch his whole body—face, hands, gestures all communicate
Don't finish sentences unless he asks you to
Offer AAC naturally—"Want to write it?" or "Show me on the tablet" without making him feel inadequate
Use yes/no questions to narrow down message ("Are you talking about tomorrow?" "Is this about Daniel?")
Value all communication—gesture, writing, AAC, and speech attempts are all valid communication
Accept approximations—if close enough to understand, acknowledge understanding
Reduce background noise—TV off, quiet space
What NOT to Do
Don't say "just relax"—relaxation doesn't fix motor planning; suggests he's not trying hard enough
Don't talk louder or simpler—his hearing and comprehension are fine
Don't rush or show impatience—increases anxiety, worsens speech
Don't pretend to understand when you don't—politely ask for clarification
Don't take over communication—let David communicate for himself
Don't avoid communication—isolation worsens depression
Don't compare to other stroke survivors—every stroke is different
Supporting AAC Use
AAC is not giving up—it's using smart tools to communicate successfully
Encourage AAC when speech isn't working—"Let's try the tablet for this one"
Respond naturally to AAC messages—focus on message, not method
Don't make him prove he tried speech first—sometimes AAC is the right choice immediately
Normalize device use—"Dad's tablet helps him talk" (to grandchildren)
Keep devices charged and accessible—don't let technology barriers prevent communication
Supporting Emotional Well-being
Acknowledge the loss—David has lost a fundamental ability; grief is appropriate
Celebrate small victories—any successful communication is worth celebrating
Include David in all conversations and decisions
Address depression—encourage professional mental health support
Maintain social connections—isolation worsens everything
Find meaning beyond speech—David has value beyond his ability to talk
Support without pity—compassion yes, pity no
Long-Term Outlook
Improvement is possible but complete recovery unlikely with severe AOS
Progress is slow—motor learning takes time, measured in months not weeks
Functional communication is achievable with AAC and strategies
Quality of life doesn't require perfect speech—meaningful participation is what matters
Family support is critical—outcomes are better with supportive communication partners
Identity can be reconstructed—David can find meaning and purpose even with persistent communication challenges
Helping Grandchildren Understand
Simple explanation: "Papa's brain was hurt and now it's hard for his mouth to make words, but he still understands everything and loves you very much"
Not their fault—if they don't understand, that's not because they did anything wrong
Teach strategies—"Papa might write it down or show you on his tablet"
Model patience—children learn from how parents respond
Encourage communication—"Papa loves when you talk to him even if he can't answer the same way"
Normalize differences—"Everyone communicates differently; Papa uses his tablet like some people use glasses"
Prognosis
Given David's age (67), severe AOS with mild Broca's aphasia, left hemisphere stroke affecting motor planning areas, excellent awareness, strong motivation, and supportive family, prognosis for functional communication improvement is fair to good.
Expected Recovery: Some spontaneous recovery expected in first 6-12 months post-stroke, but gains will be modest given severity and lesion location. Significant functional improvement possible with intensive motor-learning-based therapy. Speech will likely remain effortful and impaired, but functional communication achievable through multimodal approaches. AAC will be essential for complex communication, but practiced scripts and improved speech may allow basic verbal communication.
Positive Prognostic Factors: Strong motivation; excellent awareness and insight; supportive family willing to learn strategies; relatively preserved language (mild aphasia); intact cognition; younger end of typical AOS age range; no significant comorbidities; ability to use AAC given preserved language and literacy.
Challenges: Severity of AOS; co-occurring aphasia (even if mild); left hemisphere damage to primary speech motor planning areas; age-related reduced neural plasticity; severe depression and social withdrawal potentially limiting therapy engagement; loss of valued social roles (choir) requiring major identity adjustment; living alone increasing isolation risk.
With intensive speech therapy using motor learning principles, AAC implementation, and psychological support, David can achieve functional communication for daily needs, reduced frustration, and return to modified participation in valued activities. Natural speech recovery will be limited, but quality of life can significantly improve through multimodal communication and social reintegration.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀�␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀